EXT. FLOODED RICE PADDY, VILLAGE EDGE - DAWN

A drowned field under pewter light. Water to the knee, mud to the shin. Smoke from burned huts drifts across a line of bamboo. Distant artillery.

Three figures wade in single file. CORPORAL VAUGHN DELACROIX-AMARI (20s, helmet cocked, rifle held high) leads. Behind him, PRIVATE EZEKIEL "ZEKE" TANAKA-OLUWOLE (19, pack too large) drags a wounded man on a poncho litter. The wounded man, a SERGEANT (50s, bearded, bandaged at the thigh), has his eyes shut.

VAUGHN
Keep it low. Low.

ZEKE
I am low.

VAUGHN
You're not low, you're wet.

ZEKE
They're the same thing out here.

The Sergeant groans. Zeke stops. Vaughn turns, kneels, takes the Sergeant's wrist. Lets it go.

VAUGHN
Still with us.

ZEKE
How much farther?

VAUGHN
Treeline. Then the road.

ZEKE
You said the road an hour ago.

VAUGHN
I said the road was past the paddy.

ZEKE
This is the paddy.

VAUGHN
Then we're past the paddy soon.

A burst of automatic fire from the north. Water spouts around them. Vaughn dives into the mud. Zeke throws himself over the Sergeant. They lie there, all three, in the flooded field, faces half under.

Silence. A bird takes off. Vaughn lifts his head.

VAUGHN (CONT'D)
(whispering)
Zeke?

ZEKE
(whispering)
Yeah.

VAUGHN
You okay?

ZEKE
I'm a cushion.

The Sergeant opens one eye.

SERGEANT
Get off me.

ZEKE
Sarge!

SERGEANT
You're on my leg.

ZEKE
Sorry! Sorry!

SERGEANT
Where's the road?

VAUGHN
Treeline.

SERGEANT
Quit saying treeline. Give me a direction.

VAUGHN
East.

SERGEANT
That's the Vietcong's east or ours?

VAUGHN
Ours.

SERGEANT
Then go that way.

They rise, crouching. Zeke grabs the poncho. Vaughn takes the other corner, and the two of them haul. The Sergeant bites back a cry. Another burst of fire. They duck, and the Sergeant clamps a hand over Zeke's mouth.

SERGEANT (CONT'D)
(whispering)
Shut up, kid. That's a Pawnee.

ZEKE
(muffled)
A what?

SERGEANT
A Pawnee. It's one of ours. The twenty-three.

VAUGHN
How do you know?

SERGEANT
I've been shot by it.

The three of them listen. The firing is regular, rhythmic, and it's pointed away from them.

VAUGHN
They're clearing the treeline.

ZEKE
That's good, right?

SERGEANT
Depends who's in the treeline.

They look at each other. The artillery thunders. Smoke drifts between them.

ZEKE
Sarge, should I run?

SERGEANT
(wearily)
Son, if you run in a paddy you'll sink to your knees.

VAUGHN
We walk.

ZEKE
Together.

SERGEANT
Together. And if anyone shoots at us, we die slowly, in the proper order.

VAUGHN
Which is?

SERGEANT
The youngest dies last. So he can see it.

ZEKE
That's not funny.

SERGEANT
It wasn't a joke.

Silence. Zeke grabs his corner of the poncho. Vaughn grabs his. They haul, wading, as the sun comes up gray behind the smoke.

EXT. TREELINE - CONTINUOUS

Ahead, a figure steps out from the bamboo, rifle raised. A boy in a ragged shirt, no more than sixteen.

All three stop. Vaughn lifts his rifle. The Sergeant lifts a hand.

SERGEANT
(low)
Don't.

VAUGHN
He's armed.

SERGEANT
So are we. Look at his face.

The boy's rifle shakes. His face is wet. He says something in his own language, quiet, almost a question. Zeke lowers his own rifle and holds up one open palm.

ZEKE
(gently)
Hi.

The boy blinks. Looks at the Sergeant. At the wounded leg. He lowers the rifle to his hip and turns, slowly, and points down the road. Then he walks away into the bamboo.

VAUGHN
What was that?

SERGEANT
A road.

They resume the haul. The artillery fades.
